<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diego de Vargas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1643">1643</a>–<a href="/years/1704">1704</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diego de Vargas was a Spanish governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> who led its reconquest after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> of 1680. In 1692 he reoccupied Santa Fe largely without bloodshed, and in 1693 he returned with settlers, retaking the city by force when its Pueblo occupants resisted. He was governor again from 1703 and died on campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> in 1704.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diego-de-vargas.jpg" alt="Diego de Vargas" width="320" height="618" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ddevargas.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Diego de Vargas Zapata y Luján Ponce de León y Contreras was appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> in 1688, but he did not arrive to take up his duties until 22 February 1691. The colonists driven out by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> had been living in exile at El Paso del Norte, now Ciudad Juárez. The Spanish return was prompted by fears of French advances into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> valley and by the wish for a defensive frontier against nomadic peoples on the northern borders.</p><p>In 1692 Vargas marched on Santa Fe with a small force of soldiers and Pueblo auxiliaries, seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> and one Franciscan priest, accompanied by the Zia war captain Bartolomé de Ojeda. He promised clemency to the Pueblo people assembled there if they swore allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a> and returned to the Christian faith, and after long persuasion they agreed. Vargas wrote to the Conde de Galve, viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, that it was the thirteenth town he had reconquered in this manner. He also reached a peace with Luis Tupatu, who was made governor of thirteen villages of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>.</p><p>Vargas returned on 16 December 1693 with about 800 people, among them 100 soldiers. After the recapture of the town, the 70 Pueblo warriors who had held it were executed on 30 December, and about 400 women and children of their families were sentenced to ten years' servitude among the colonists. In 1696 fourteen pueblos rose again, killing five missionaries and thirty-four settlers, and Vargas's retribution was severe and prolonged. Many Pueblo people fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a> or onto the Great Plains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Vargas had prayed to the Virgin Mary as La Conquistadora for a peaceful re-entry, and the feast he held in her honour continues as the Fiestas de Santa Fe. A re-enactment of his entry, The Entrada, drew protests from Native Americans from 2015 and was later removed from the fiesta. On 18 June 2020 the city removed his statue, which was afterwards displayed at the New Mexico History Museum. Like many wealthy subjects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, Vargas owned slaves; he freed his coachman Ignacio de la Cruz.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diego de Vargas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the northern frontier, a line of presidios, or garrisoned forts, protected missions and mining towns against Apache and Comanche raids, and the Pueblo Revolt of 1680 drove the Spaniards out of New Mexico until <b>Diego de Vargas</b> returned in 1692.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Pueblos endured tribute, forced labour and the suppression of their religion, and on 10 August 1680 they rose in the Pueblo Revolt led by Popé, killing about 400 colonists and 21 Franciscan friars and driving the Spanish from New Mexico until <b>Diego de Vargas</b> reconquered it from 1692.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ancestral-puebloans">Ancestral Puebloans · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pueblo Revolt of 1680, led by Popé, drove the Spanish out until <b>Diego de Vargas</b> returned in 1692.</blockquote><a href="/state/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Santa Fe de Nuevo Mexico</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Bernalillo, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1682" aria-label="Madrid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Diego de Vargas"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4759 4110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4759 4110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1682">What was here in 1682? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
